47470733a2f2f61727869762e6f7267/pdf/2305.07759.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"a tiny of tiny stories\",\"url\":\"https://internal-mongodb.champds.com/archive-ga-23-03/a-tiny-of-tiny-stories.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"docs.google.com\",\"url\":\"https://docs.google.[scrubbed:long-base64]?format=pdf\"}]\n\nTinyStories is a synthetic dataset of short children's stories from Ronen Eldan and Yuanzhi Li at Microsoft Research, introduced in a 2023 paper. The paper asks how small a language model can be and still produce coherent English.\n\n- **The dataset:** The authors built it with GPT-3.5 and GPT-4, and the stories use vocabulary that 3- to 4-year-olds would typically understand (a synthetic dataset of short stories that are intended to contain only words that most 3 to 4-year-old children would typically understand). The dataset is on Hugging Face under roneneldan/TinyStories.\n- **Why it matters:** The paper reports that models with around 125M parameters, like GPT-2 small, often struggle to produce coherent text beyond a few words. TinyStories models are much smaller, with some under 10 million parameters, and still produce fluent text (below 10 million total parameters).\n- **Evaluation:** The authors use GPT-4 to grade model outputs as if a teacher were scoring student stories, with separate scores for grammar, creativity, and consistency.\n- **Released models:** Pretrained models include TinyStories-1M, 3M, 9M, 28M, and 33M, plus Instruct variants on Hugging Face.\n- **Follow-on work:** Later projects such as SimpleStories cite TinyStories as a starting point. One source notes that TinyStories is a well received dataset of ca. 2M model-generated short stories in simple English, and that the original contains some grammatical errors.\n\nThe search also returned unrelated results, such as a children's-book series and a writing game, which are not the same TinyStories dataset.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]